.TH GROUP 4 "1 November 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
group \- the group file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/group
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each line of
.B /etc/group
is one group, four fields separated by colons:
.PP
.RS
.IB name : password : gid : members
.RE
.PP
.I name
is the group's name,
.I password
is empty,
.I gid
its number and
.I members
the logins that have the group besides their own (their
.B /etc/passwd
group), separated by commas.
.PP
The base package ships the system's own file with the groups the add-on
packages need added; a package that installs a file or device node owned by
a group can be installed only when the group is in this file.
.TP
.B audio
(gid 20) the sound device: its node is readable and writable by root and by
the members of
.BR audio .
.SH ADDING A LOGIN TO A GROUP
.B usermod \-G
sets a login's whole list of extra groups, so name the ones it has as well;
.B groups
.I login
prints them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
groups jane
usermod -G audio jane
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.PP
for a login with no other extra groups, or
.B "usermod \-G audio,games jane"
when it is in
.B games
too.
A new login gets the group with
.BR "useradd \-G audio" .
The change takes effect at the login's next login.
.SH UPDATES
The file is a configuration file of the base package: an update never
overwrites a file that was changed (a group added, a member added), nor,
on a system installed before the base package carried this file, the file
it finds.
It keeps the file and writes the new default as
.BR /etc/group.new ,
and the package tools list it as pending.
When the new default only adds a group, add it to the kept file with
.BR groupadd ,
for example
.B "groupadd \-g 20 audio"
(any free number will do: packages name the group, not its number), then
remove
.BR /etc/group.new .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/group
.TP
.B /etc/group.new
a new default waiting to be merged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR groupadd (1M),
.BR useradd (1M),
.BR usermod (1M),
.BR groups (1)
